\f1 Note: This document generally follows the conventions of the \ul\i P?S/8\par
Keyboard Monitor Command Guide\ulnone\i0  available separately.\par
\par
This document will describe the various methods of executing PDP-8 assembly language programs using the P?S/8\super [1]\nosupersub  operating system. Additionally, specific command examples will be provided for the relevant command variants.  Some familiarity with PDP-8 assembly language programming is required, but P?S/8 users of any skill level can benefit from this information.\line\line In some cases, comparisons will be drawn with the DEC \i OS/\i0 8\super [2]\nosupersub  operating system, which has a checkered history and is in some ways a "bastard step-cousin" of P?S/8.  (Additional information is available below.)\par
\par
P?S/8 was written independently of DEC by members of the P?S\super [1]\nosupersub  over a course of decades.  (It is still undergoing further development as of this writing.)\par
\par
Note: There is a lone exception to the statement above: Rick Merrill of DEC created \ul\i FOCAL, 1969\ulnone  \i0 as a console terminal-only interactive calculator program (and language) with very limited paper-tape support for input (as a frill).  FOCAL, 1969 is a binary base component of the system program known as P?S/8 \i FOCAL\i0 .  FOCAL includes many additional features, making it an effective component of the P?S/8 environment.  This is accomplished while maintaining internal binary compatibility with the original DEC release; this is an important aspect of the FOCAL system program allowing virtually all programs and patches (written by members of the FOCAL, 1969 user community at large) to run as intended.\par
\par
Earlier efforts.\par
\par
Quite significantly, many original members of the DEC PDP-8 programming staff started their PDP-8 programming careers on the immediate forebear of P?S/8, known as \ul\i The R-L Monitor System\ulnone\i0  (or \i MS/8\i0 , the name used on its submission to \i DECUS).\i0   DECUS was the usual repository for DEC-compatible programs either not supported by DEC or written by users.\par
\par
Some of these individuals were the actual implementers of The R-L Monitor System (which was demonstrated to DEC management personnel, all of whom were clueless and incapable of understanding it at a fundamental level).  (Additionally, some of the same programmers were involved in the original demo program that eventually became OS/8.)\par
\par
The R-L Monitor System was created out of whole cloth by hand program assembly; the subsequent binary instructions were manually entered into memory using the front panel of a specific PDP-8 located in the Electrical Engineering department of the Polytechnic Institute of Brooklyn (now a division of New York University).  The project was undertaken by the five original of the WCFMPG\super [1]\nosupersub .\par
\par
This work was done during the four month period just before the fall semester of 1967 at Brooklyn Poly; all of the relevant people indicated in this document met each other there (but not in the academic sense of classmates, but rather because the PDP-8 in question was available to any students who had an interest in the machine).\line\line Had a prior facility such as the R-L Monitor (or P?S/8) pre-existed, the entire effort would likely have been accomplished in about a quarter of the time.  This is due to the extreme talent of the five individuals who collaborated on this project [which came about when the PDP-8 was upgraded to include a TC01 DECtape controller and TU55 DECtape drive).  Unfortunately, this fantasy never happened, and it is an extremely tedious and error prone process to turn programming in the heads of a small group of individuals into a working product without any programming tools in the conventional sense.  (This is not uncommon when referring to pioneering efforts in computing; and these individuals were exceptional.)\line\line While this mixed group of friends and colleagues worked together and separately and freely discussed the merits of PDP-8 operating system techniques, there came a time when there was at first only a geographical "split" in that the original five individuals were simultaneously hired by DEC soon after finishing The R-L Monitor System; as such, little if any of the actual source code was preserved, and only a few items were recreated, primarily devoid of comments.  As such, much of the R-L Monitor System is undocumented on many levels.\line\line Less than two years later, several of the other students (including the author of this document) decided to review the "innards" of The\par
R-L Monitor System which led to the creation of sparse documentation included with the MS/8 DECUS submission.  It is notable that, by that time, Charles Lasner (author of this document) had added on the last system program to the R-L system, an adaptation of FOCAL, 1969 that was the precursor to P?S/8 FOCAL; this was included in the DECUS submission in the form of R-L text files which includes some comments.  Unfortunately, the R-L Monitor system supports an inadequate text file format that discourages commenting.\line\line Note: This was one of the many reasons, we, as a group, decided to upgrade the R-L Monitor System; P?S/8 as it exists today is the direct result of these endeavors, still being worked on over 50 years later as of this writing.  Many improvements later, P?S/8 is a far more viable general-purpose operating system for the same exact hardware as the minimum complement of required hardware, with support for virtually all devices that became available over the years.\par
\par
Note: The R-L Monitor System only supports the original hardware configuration as described above; it is a closed-ended system.\line\line Unfortunately, there was another level of "split" among these groups sometime later because of DEC management interference at a profound level based on what can only be referred to as abject stupidity and arrogance.  Management was seeking a new operating system to be written by the then-current programming staff.  It is a maxim of programming in general: Programmers write code, and managers manage the programmers, not the programs they write.  Unfortunately, the managers at DEC were clueless as to this basic concept.  Here are the relevant issues:\line\line 1) They saw themselves as hardware and software and marketing\par
   directors.  As such, the phrase "jack of all trades, master of\par
   none" applies to this situation.\par
\par
2) They had an agenda to arbitrarily require 8K of memory instead of\par
   the obvious rational decision:  The operating system's minimum\par
   memory requirement should be irrelevant.  It might be helpful in\par
   certain applications to allow a subset of the system to \b only\par
   require \b0 4K of memory; that said, it would \b possibly\b0  be recommended\par
   that to make use of what they proposed, perhaps 8K (or more) memory\par
   should be strongly \b recommended\b0 .  This concept of this material\par
   difference was figuratively over their heads.  They just insisted\par
   the product had to require 8K -- case closed --.\par
\par
   Note: The proposed system still could have been marketed as an 8K\par
   product (as it was, eventually named OS/8); in fact, later due to\par
   mediocre design, in some cases it eventually required 12K.  This\par
   still doesn't change the fact that it could be useful to certain\par
   customers if it could run in 4K.  DEC later sold many different\par
   configurations of PDP-8/A models that in some cases had \b less\b0  than\par
   4K memory.  Marketing of those machines could have easily benefited\par
   by recommending 4K memory modules.  Ironically, during that era,\par
   P?S/8 was demonstrated to one of these managers and their staff.\par
   Despite the obvious advantages, P?S/8 was rejected merely because\par
   it \b could\b0  run in 4K of memory (depending on configuration) while at\par
   the same time OS/8 was marketed including the 8K/12K requirement.\par
\par
   A far more rational argument can be made that describes the long-\par
   term goal of P?S/8:\line\line    P?S/8 requires 4K of memory (or perhaps 8K depending on the\par
   hardware configuration); however, depending on specific needs, more\par
   memory might be indicated, perhaps as much as 32K.  A reasonable\par
   assertion can be made that \b development\b0  of a program could require\par
   more memory, but the binary product of that development may perhaps\par
   \b execute\b0  in 4K (this is often the case).  (The 4K boundaries arise\par
   due to the nature of the PDP-8 architecture.)\par
\par
3) A truly awful system (known as \i The Disk Monitor System\i0  on DECtape)\par
   had been created at DEC some time after The R-L Monitor System was\par
   already heavily used at Brooklyn Poly and other sites.  The R-L\par
   Monitor System was deployed by certain members of the P?S who used\par
   the software to allow them to create programs at their places of\par
   employment.\line\line    This rather pathetic system also requires 4K, but is quite poor\par
   when compared to P?S/8, yet these foolhardy managers insisted on\par
   recreating some of the awful internal features of this horrible\par
   system; in reality, proper design makes these notions idiotic and\par
   quite obsolete.\line\line    Despite this folly, the managers insisted these notions be included\par
   in the new project that was being demonstrated to them by the same\par
   programmers who had written The R-L Monitor System.\line\line    The writer of the demonstration acquiesced to the 8K requirement,\par
   but only out of expediency as opposed to technical reasons\super [2]\nosupersub .\par
\par
