This is a preliminary specification for a new P?S/8 utility to be known as \i BINCON\i0 .\par
\par
\f0\fs20\par
\fs24 P?S/8 program execution methods.\par
\par
Program execution generally applies to programs already included in the System  Programs Directory, as the user files in the Tiny File System [TFS] directory are  limited to text and binary files [other than a few assorted oddities].\par
\par
Binary files are optimized for loading into memory by the "Slurp" loader using a  format that approaches 6/7 the efficiency of core-image binary, yet is actually  random-access loading as opposed to loading complete blocks.\par
\par
The basic Slurp loader has no options; the program is loaded into memory and has to  fend for itself once started.  The only action available is to exit to location  07600 to reload P?S/8 via the provided bootstrap.\par
\par
Note: The definition of the bootstrap in this context is the contents of at most  07600-07642 which was placed there by the Slurp loading process, generally as some  part of the loader code that also aids in the bootup process.  In the next release,  this will be scaled back slightly to 07600-07641 to allow one additional word to be  added to the P?S/8 variant of the BIN loader, which can eliminate a small  theoretical weakness associated with loading programs for the TC58 3-cycle data  break version of the .5" magnetic tape controller, etc.  There are commands in\par
P?S/8 BIN to load a version of the BIN and RIM loaders along with the same  bootstrap as used at the start of the Slurp loader.  To date, it appears the  longest boot code is actually the TC01/TC08 version which is actually an additional  word shorter than the new shorter upper limit; as such, no problems are  contemplated making this change.  The next release of the BIN utility will cover  this and other issues addressed in Version 9A; Version 8Z, the present stable  release, is subject to this theoretical problem.\par
\par
Note:  P?S/8, unlike OS/8, also runs on DECmates will full support.  However,  console programming must conform to the incompatible hardware.  While a program can  detect this situation itself, it is recommended that a program be made to be more  conforming with additional P?S/8 requirements before attempting this.  For all  PDP-8, PDP-12 and related models, this is not an issue, and any reasonable form of  console handling is allowed.\par
\par
Note: P?S/8 supplies the program, when initially loaded, with the certainty the  console output flag is either up or will be soon after starting.  Thus, it is  recommended to use the overlapped method of console output to take advantage of  this.  If the program is restarted, this cannot be guaranteed as the new context is  the responsibility of the program itself now.  [Fortunately, few programs are meant  to be restarted; however, there are rules regarding restart and consistent handling  of the console output flag.]\par
\par
In this environment described by Slurp loading, there are no system facilities  whatsoever.\par
\par
Accessing certain P?S/8 system parameters.\par
\par
The first cut at improving this situation is to load the program with either the /I  or /V options.\par
\par
/I - After the program is loaded, reload the system device handler.  This sounds  counter-intuitive and impossible.  Yet, it does work.  Advanced PDP-8 programmers  who do not understand this can have their conventional wisdom notions erased from  their brains.  Only those who REALLY understand PDP-8 storage devices can  understand this, but the point is, if you believe this cannot be done, I have a  bridge in Brooklyn to sell you :-).\par
\par
There is a modest restriction on the use of the /I option switch:  The P?S/8 BIN   utility can load up to 17 [0021 octal] files normally.  When /I is in effect, this  is lowered by one to 16 [0020 octal].  The reason is that a special "file" is  appended onto the passed list of files to be loaded, to perform the dark grey magic  to make the overall loading work as advertised.  [The special "file" can be  described as a "friendly virus".  The exact mechanism is device-dependent, but the  overall effect is identical: Once the "file" is loaded, the system device handler  is viable and the program can be started at the designated starting address in the  designated starting field.]\par
\par
This restores the ability to access the P?S/8 system device handler call at 07640  and following, etc.  However, this is the only system services available in this  usage [but perhaps the program only requires this and nothing else].  Note: Future  releases could expand on this, but there is no current commitment because every  device that this pertains to must be thoroughly tested, etc.  At best, only  information known to be written on the system device can be conceptually stored; as  such, this might aspire to the system date value as implemented in the next  release.\par
\par
/V - While the Slurp loader [with or without /I] replaces the system device  handler, and as such, that form of loading is the fastest [and can be performed  with the device write-locked], there is always an alternative method of loading.  This option forces the use of a virtual loader that is vaguely analogous to the  operations of the OS/8 ABSLDR program, etc.  On certain devices, it is not possible  to support a hardware-dependent Slurp loader due to hardware limitations.  On such  system, /V becomes mandatory and is internally forced on accordingly; in these  cases, the /I option switch is ignored.\par
\par
The advantage of this mode is that the entire system kernel is present; all system  services can be referenced to allow programs to completely conform to P?S/8  standards if desired.  The virtual loader requires write-enable on the system  device where applicable, as part of the loading process.\par
\par
As a practical matter, the only additional information gained by this method is  access to the system date word.  In P/S/8 Version 8Z, the date word is contained in  07610 and is a 12-bit value that covers a period of approximately 11 years.  A  non-resident parameter must be adhered to that changes every 8 years with an  additional 3 year grace period before the parameter update must be deployed.\par
\par
While not actually present in the system, the value of the parameter is as follows:\par
\par
[Note: This is always a four-bit value and will be shown in bit form.]\par
\par
0000 = the base year for the date is 1960.  Thus, if this base year group value is  used, the system date pertains to the years 1960-1971.\par
\par
Note: OS/8 can only handle the system date in a TRUSTWORTHY manner from 1970  through 1977.  The actual year may be off by 8 or 16 or 24 years and can only be  reckoned by intimate knowledge of each file to achieve certainty; this is an  unfixable limitation of OS/8 that can be troublesome, especially when creating new  files that are limited to this primitive facility.\par
\par
1960 was chosen as the base year for several reasons:  Actual work on the earliest  forebears of P?S/8 started prior to 1968; additionally, 12-bit computing itself  started in 1960 with the CDC 160 design of Seymour Cray.  The earliest  implementations of the date value were thus tested during the actual years of the  design.\par
\par
0001 - 1968-1979.  Back in the day, this was clearly superior to OS/8, which became  an ugly kludge in 1978.  Warnings were issued by users as far back as 1975, but  OS/8's design is too fixated to create a proper solution.  One of the long-term  goals of the P?S/8 project is to completely obsolete the OS/8 system while  providing a new "home" for worthwhile programs that currently only work from OS/8,  etc.  This will be addressed in detail once more of the P?S/8 SHELL is functional.\par
\par
0002 - 1976-1987.\par
\par
0003 - 1984-1995.\par
\par
0004 - 1992-2003.  Note: The P?S/8 DATE utility includes the notion of the favored  century short-hand.  This is a more effective solution than that of the typical Y2K  problems because  you can designate which are the years that two-digit input is  allowed; full four digit is required for all other potential years; most Y2K  solutions abandoned the former entirely and forced the use of the latter; by  "sliding" the two-digit shorthand forward, the notion could be continued for the  benefit of most users.\par
\par
0005 - 2000-2011.\par
\par
0006 - 2008-2019.  Note: Certain nearly-complete releases of P?S/8 exist with this  setting.  These systems should be updated as the date will expire next year!\par
\par
0007 - 2016-2027 - The relevant locations on the P/?S/8 TC01/TC08 release tape have  been patched to this value.  A complete list is available in the system patch log  file.  This technique can be continued until 2099 when the code fails due to a  longstanding bug; however, this is of no consequence as this method of date group  reckoning is being abandoned.\par
\par
To unpack the date information for Version 8Z, the date word should be divided by  372 [this is the number of days in an idealized year where every month has 31  days].  The quotient is the relative year [to be added to the imputed base group as  defined above].  The remainder is the relative day of the year.  Dividing that by  12 yields the relative month 0-11 past January.  The remainder is the relative day  of the month [which becomes the numbered day of the month if you add one].  These  values can be used with the more general reckoning of time to determine day of the  week, etc.  Due to a bug in the code used throughout the system, century years are  not handled properly past 2099.\par
\par
Starting in Version 9A, the date implementation is being overhauled.  The new date  format is 18 bits where bits[1-6] of the contents of location 07611 are the six  highest bits; the contents of 07610 remain as the low-order 12-bits but the new  format is the 18-bit calculation of the unique day calculated nearly linearly.   Thus, no further patching will be required.  Within the 12 low-order bits, the  high-order 3 bits are the relative year within the 8-year group defined by the bits  in 07611.  The next 4 bits are the relative month and the low-order 5 bits are the  relative day in the month.  To allow the system to be truly undated, the relative  day of the month is origin 1; as such, if the 12-bit word is all zeroes, the system  date has not been set.\par
 \par
Note:  The low-order 12-bits are essentially the same as in OS/8 except the bit  weighting is not scrambled as in OS/8.  As such, it is possible to more readily  sort dates of files without having to bit twiddle the data first.  This is one of  the many sore spot issues of OS/8 caused by the complete folly of using demo code  instead of developed code, etc.\par
\par
If bits[1-6] are isolated and moved to bits[6-11], the two words form an 18-bit  integer that can be readily handled in double-precision arithmetic.  This is the  offset past the year 1900 thus, that constant value of the starting year has to be  added to create the proper date in the range of 01-Jan-1900 through 31-Dec-2411.\par
\par
Additional P?S/8 system kernel information.\par
\par
To get access to more of the available system data, there is a way to write out the  program to a core-image without dealing with the inner workings of the Systems  Program directory.  While it is necessary to learn how to interact with the  parameters themselves, dealing with the overall Systems Program directory is an  additional involved process; at the least, the former can be mastered without  learning the latter and all of the attendant considerations.\par
\par
Note: While it is true the following is somewhat of a copout, few users of P?S/8  will aspire to creating their own PERMANENT system programs that should be included  in the System Programs directory [perhaps coupled with removing certain programs  already present] since this is a resource that must be managed with precision.   Additionally, the System Programs directory offers additional features that are  only used currently by a small few existing portions of a typical P?S/8 system:\par
\par
1) System programs can be passed the =xxxx construct if present on a command line.   Any value between 0 and 4094 [0000 through 7776 octal] can be used.  If the  parameter is not present, the value defaults to 4095 [7777 octal], the program can  decide some specific default action.\par
\par
While this feature per se is available to all programs [even the easily used subset  described below], there is a system attribute to change the way decimal radix  values are handled:\par
\par
The default system parsing is to force the =xxxx to be interpreted in octal.  If  this special attribute is passed, the method is changed to interpret the parameter  in the current radix setting [which defaults to decimal and is easily changed  either temporarily or permanently].  This parameter is not available in the  alternate method to be described below.\par
\par
2) Generally, the additional information on the command line is parsed according to  all of the rules associated with command formation.  A system attribute is  available that prevents parsing of what potentially would be the file name section  of a command; this is used by overlays to the P?S/8 keyboard monitor to take  advantage of existing routines within the monitor, etc.  In these circumstances,  the rest of the line is not actually filename-oriented; the program determines the  proper syntax particulars and parses the rest of the command-line according to its  own rules.\par
\par
For example, the P?S/8 SET utility program uses its own rules to parse the command  line to set various system parameters. Moreover, this is meaningless outside of the  specific environment of interacting with the keyboard monitor [or at least most of  it; there are ways to reload a portion or not] and is only relevant to overlay or  at least adjacent memory loading.  This restriction can only be accomplished by  access to the System Programs directory to indicate only partial loading of memory  that creates the special environment to use this attribute.  While this is not  likely of much use to any P?S/8 user short of an implementer, it is none-the-less  only available to system programs fully described in the System Programs directory,  and is thus an advanced feature of little use to general program development.\par
\par
3) The file scan attribute has a related additional attribute created solely for  the BSAVE command [or in theory something akin to it].\par
\par
The issue is that the BSAVE is used as follows:\par
\par
BSAVE requires a list of output files such as OUT1, OUT2, OUT3 etc. which we will  summarize as outfiles.  Memory locations to be saved are specified as groups of  contiguous memory locations anywhere within the 32K address space; individual  memory locations can also be saved.  We will summarize this as memlocs.\par
\par
Thus, the BSAVE command can be formed as follows:\par
\par
.BSAVE outfiles < memlocs\par
\par
OR\par
\par
.BSAVE memlocs > outfiles\par
\par
Note: All P?S/8 execution-class commands have this "bidirectional" parsing  characteristic; this allows the user to use whichever is more familiar, as other  systems are known to form commands one way or another.  P?S/8 does not take "sides"  in such issues and commands are formed either way.\par
\par
Thus, output files must be allowed and input file parsing must be prevented.  Both  this attribute as well as the one described above in 2) are unavailable in the  simplified form.\par
\par
4) P?S/8 in the basic system form [SHELL disabled], supports the concept of  overlays.  The initial program segment is loaded and started at an address  determined by the System Programs directory entry.  Programs designed to support  overlays determine the particulars of where they were loaded from and then  calculate hard block count offsets to where the overlays are as determined by  program design.\par
\par
For example, when the PAL assembler is loaded, once-only initialization code  determines the starting block of the internal symbol table which is written out in  compacted form.  This code reads the table into an available buffer [later used to  read in the source text] and "sprays" each symbol into dynamically allocated memory  for the symbol table.  This is necessary as the PAL program uses address hashing  for rapid lookup of each symbol.  Using this method, even the permanent symbol  table is properly hash-loaded and there are no exceptions, etc.  At the end of the  assembly, another overlay is loaded to produce a well-formatted statistical report  of the particulars during the assembly process [number of errors, count of  generated links, count of symbols, and a synthetic count of memory used to aid in  determining if there is enough memory to assemble the program in the future; this  is important for very large assemblies, additionally there are optimization  switches to change the amount of memory allocated to buffers to allow additional  symbols if necessary, but at the expense of overall assembly speed and performance,  etc.].\par
\par
P?S/8 FOCAL uses overlays to change the way FOCAL operates. Initially, what LATER  becomes command input from the console is redirected to the contents of text files  passed to FOCAL.  Once overall end of all text files is reached, an overlay is used  to restore keyboard input, etc.\par
\par
Another overlay is used to handle the unimplemented L[ibrary] command that causes  the default action one sees in the paper-tape version.  Even that fully compatible  minor output indicates the relative use of internal resources and can be useful if  the FOCAL program and/or variables and/or stack depth is getting close to the  limit, etc.  Future releases might support expansion of the L command to include  loading and/or saving of FOCAL programs to files.  This is open-ended at this point  and could be expanded upon in the future.  However, the foundation of the overlay  structure is already present.\par
\par
The only way to take advantage of this is with complete mastery of a variety of\par
P?S/8 internal resources, and perhaps only a small handful of users may ever need  to have such depth of understanding.\par
\par
Note: The overlay feature is quite flexible and powerful, and well advanced beyond  OS/8, it will be recreated in the SHELL design to allow overlay structures within  individual core-image executables.\par
\par
5) The System Programs directory allows the setting of custom program starting  addresses stated within the directory.  The methodology below has restrictions on  this as well.\par
\par
Using the GET command.\par
\par
A unique feature of P?S/8 is the placement of two key files near the physical start  of the device, specifically known as % and $ respectively.  These are otherwise  "illegal" names with regard to the valid file name characters of files contained in  the TFS directory, but are treated as specific special cases.  This allows  transient usage for small projects, with the understanding that the contents may be  destroyed by certain operations, but can be helpful to produce results rapidly.   Numerous examples can be found elsewhere, but this is outside of the bounds of this  discussion:\par
\par
The Slurp loader [and the virtual equivalent] supports the ability to have present  access to the system device handler under specific circumstances.  If these  requirements are followed, starting at location 07632 invokes the embedded call to  the system device handler with arguments set to save all of field 0 in blocks 0020  through 0057 on the system boot device.  These are precisely the blocks of the %  and $ files aggregated together.  [And as such, this usage is incompatible with  those outlined above, which is why they are described as fleeting and transient,  etc.]\par
\par
Note: If the need arises, the two files % and $ can be copies to named TFS files  for deferred usage at another time.  This includes debugging the program with P?S/8  ODT which is compatible with this core-image mechanism.  Alternatively, specific  memory locations can be saved to Slurp binary files using BSAVE; however, the  entire contents of field 0 requires three binary output files while just preserving  the image format requires two files [and no overhead to reload other than the  restoration of the two files].\par
\par
In general, P?S/8 does not use extended memory, thus after any form of binary  loading, extended memory will remain intact [until a program is run that  deliberately changes this, such as another assembly or other program that checks  for and takes advantage of extended memory].  By staying within definable bounds,  all extended memory locations will remain intact, etc.\par
\par
Since now the entirety of field 0 has been saved as a virtual core image, several  programs can interact with this, such as P?S/8 ODT.  Changes made by ODT are thus  permanent if in field 0.  The BSAVE command can save the locations to be stated by  the user back into stated output files, etc.\par
\par
After a GET command, it is possible to use the START command.  All program  parameters can be specified, including input and output files, the =xxxx parameter  and all command-line switches from /A through /Z and /0 through /9.\par
\par
However, the START command reserves the use of the /0 through /7 switch to allow  starting the program in extended memory.  Additionally, the value of the =xxxx  construct is used as the starting address [00200 if none given].\par
\par
Thus, this is a nearly acceptable way to run the equivalent of a system program  without any interaction with the System Programs directory.\par
\par
Additional execution.\par
\par
As of P?S/8 Version 8Y, there is an alternate mechanism:\par
\par
Execution is often accomplished in P?S/8 by the use of the R command.  [Presently  the RU[n] command is entirely equivalent, but in theory could change in future  releases].\par
\par
If the program name is given as either % or $, this has special meaning to the  execution commands:\par
\par
The program becomes the virtual image of field 0.  Blocks 0020-0057 are loaded into  00000-07577 and started at 00200.  All of the parameters specified apply to using  the program just created recently with the GET command, etc.\par
\par
Of course, the attributes stated above are not possible, including that the =xxxx  command cannot be stated in the current radix, but rather must be interpreted as  octal digits [as are most programs in the system program directory].\par
\par
Thus, for most users, this is a perfectly viable way to perform all P?S/8 system  kernel interactions beyond these few minor restrictions without interaction with  the System Programs directory.\par
\par
Additional available information from the system kernel.\par
\par
07604- The passed command-line switches /A through /L.  This is completely  equivalent to OS/8 [which imitated P?S/8 which in turn imitates TOPS10].\par
07605 - /M-X.\par
07605 - /Y- /9.\par
\par
As in OS/8, the lowest ranking bit is the high-order and the bits are reckoned left  to right.  Thus, the effort in parsing command-line bits is a virtual "tie" in  terms of using them in P?S/8 versus OS/8 [but are defined in specifically different  memory locations within the kernel.]\par
\par
OS/8 requires a large amount of interaction with the file system to successfully  use any files.  The P?S/8 SHELL will be somewhat similar, yet also more powerful.   However, in the basic P?S/8 system, things are quite a bit easier:\par
\par
07607 - this is a shared word:\par
\par
Bit[0] is a SHELL parameter that is not relevant at this time, it is reserved to  determine the exact placement of the area in the SHELL system that has all of the  file "stuff" that any aspect of the system needs to know, etc.  This is far more  comprehensive than OS/8, in part because of the open-ended nature of this area as  follows:\par
\par
a) It is 256 words long.  The closest equivalent in OS/8 is 64 words or so but  encompasses far less information.\par
\par
b) It includes the name of the latest executable program [a common concept in other  systems but not implemented in OS/8].  Loading considerations have temporaries  reserved within the 256 words; much of this space is available for future needs.\par
\par
c) File names in OS/8 are in the 6.2 notation where the TOPS-10 and RT-11 filenames  are 6.3.  MS-DOS names are  8.3.  P?S/8 SHELL file names are 12.4 and include case  retention the same way as OS/2.  [Case retention means that when a file is created,  the case of all alpha characters' initial values are determined.  However, for all  file lookup purposes, the case is ignored.  Utilities can be written to change this  if desired, to standardize on any desired scheme, such as auto-capitalize lower- case the rest, all lower-case, all upper-case, any form of mixed case or custom,  etc.  Utilities that create directory printouts can choose to override this for  cosmetic purposes.  This is a feature that adds flexibility and is conceptually  beyond OS/8, etc.\par
\par
As a practical matter, this means that source files do not have to be known as, for  example, PROGRM.PA and instead can easily be PROGRAM.PAL or Program.PAL etc.  Core  image files do not have to be PROGRM.SV but rather PROGRAM.SAVE, etc. [or perhaps  PROGRAM.EXEC.]  Fortan files can be MYPROGRAM.FORT and TECO macros can be  MyMacro.TECO.  Use your imagination instead of being forced to use cryptic short  names limited to 6 characters, etc.\par
\par
Thus, in the SHELL the latest file name will be six words of packed text characters  plus a separate word for each of the 12 characters' case select bit.  Searches will  ignore the case word used only when creating the file initially.  [A monitor call  can be implemented to RENAME with perhaps changing only the case bits word in the  directory entry.]\par
\par
Since P?S/8 devices use logical units, logical devices will be known as up to four  characters and SEPARATELY logical units up to 0 through 7.  Thus, a DTA handler  that can access DTA0: through DTA7: doesn't tax this notation, it could be such as  TAPE0: through TAPE7: or somesuch.  [The syntax could be TAPE:0 through TAPE:7 if  desired; the four characters of the overall name of the device is unrelated to the  logical unit 0 through 7.]\par
\par
All file operations will be centralized to this area.  It will be implemented in  one of two possibe ways:\par
\par
1) If enough memory is available, this will be located in x5400-x5777 where x is  the highest field of memory available [at least 8K or at most 32K].  Clearly, it is  highly recommended to have extended memory to accommodate this.  Note:  P?S/8 DOES  NOT automatically reserve ANY extended memory field; compare this to OS/8 that also  reserves field 1 17600-17777.  15-bit addressing programs such as OS/8-type BASIC  implementations and Fortran IV require linear contiguous 15-bit addressing and in  OS/8 this can never be any less than 20000 because of all the overhead [and that  itself is accomplished by ugly kludges!].  But in a P?S/8 "port" of the Fortran IV  runtime system, this could ALWAYS start at 10000 onward, etc.\par
\par
The reason these addresses were chosen is as follows:\par
\par
a) P?S/8 system device handlers MAY require x6000-x7777 due to hardware  restrictions.  In general, this follows the fule of thumb of OS/8, thus for  example, OS/8 requires 12K to support the RX01 and TD8E instead of the usual 8K  while P?S/8 will require 8K to suport these devices instead of the usual 4K.\par
\par
As such, if this area [known as the File and Communications area [FCA] is memory  resident, it is just "behind" the system device handler extension area [if  implemented].\par
\par
In such a configuration, dynamic memory allocation in conforming programs can  determine if the highest field of memory exists as a FULL field, or a PARTIAL field  ending at either 5377 or 5777 depending.  In the case of linear 15-bit addressing,  this would define the upper limit for a compiled binary load image, etc.\par
\par
b) P?S/8 supports the concept [conceptually beyond OS/8] of the Logical Console  Overlay.  If this is implemented, it occupies x0000-x5377.  As such, if active, the  entirety of the highest field of memory may be fully occupied, etc.\par
\par
If the Logical Console Overlay is enabled, a conforming program dynamically  reconfigures itself to perform logical subroutine calls instead of physical input  and output.\par
\par
For example:\par
\par
Typical console input routine [simplified]\par
\par
        KSF\par
        JMP    ,-1\par
        KRB\par
\par
If the overlay is in effect, change the code to the following:\par
\par
        CIF     70\par
\tab JMS     31\par
        JMP     .-2\par
\par
Generally the calling field is usually field 0, but the conforming program must set  this accurately [any field is allowed].  the CIF xx to the field xx is determined  elsewhere [see below].\par
\par
Location 31 is the address designated for the logical console input.\par
\par
If keyboard input is available, the subroutine call skips and takes the skip return  with the AC loaded with the latest character from the logical device.  [Note: The  Logical Console Overlay can be configured separately for a whole host of devices.   Practical implementations include the VT8E keyboard or alternate serial ports  identical to the 03/04 console except perhaps being device 40/41 to an alternate  terminal, etc.  This is only the concern of the Overlay which has a self- configuration internal routine and designated binary patch files to change the  configuration.  Most devices have already been written, but a few are only in the  planning stages [such as no change on the keyboard, but output to the PDP-12  VR12/14 scope display].  Many notes are already available on that implementation,  but it must be developed on a working PDP-12, etc.  The VT8E is not a terminal,  although it physically resembles a VT05 actual terminal; however, the display is an  extremely fast DMA device and the VT8E configuration includes essentially a  terminal emulator package, etc.\par
\par
Similarly, calls to location 33 are for output.  Skip return means the output  device accepted the input character.  Internally, this could also mean buffering is  being performed, but this is device-dependent, and as such, is "invisible" to the  caller, etc.\par
\par
Two more calls exist to replace the standard device 66 lineprinter which also  logically supports a reverse channel from the [serial] printer where Control- S/Control-Q protocol may be required.\par
\par
However, it is NOT necessary for non-interrupt programming to call the reverse  channel routine.  When output is logically directed to lineprinter output, the  Overlay checks the assocated input hardware and automatically updates in sync with  the hardware, always attempting to maintain the buffer no more than 3/4 full and  more desirably as empty as possible, but when 1/4 full, will indicate more  characters can [now] be sent.  But all characters are buffered inside of the  Overlay; failng to do this, the call will NOT take the skip return.\par
\par
This method drives line printers to near interrupt-driven speed and has been  demonstrated on a 1200 LPM printer to be FOUR TIMES AS FAST as OS/8 which  essentially wastes any printer capability past 300 LPM due to profoundly poor  design.\par
\par
There is also a call to location 0000 [done on purpose to mimic the hardware  interrupt] to poll the logical devices.  Interrupt-driven programs should do this  before checking for the corresponding physical devices, calling the appropriate  device calls as the logical flags dictate.  P?S/8 FOCAL is a good example of a  program that hooks into the logical interrupt system, etc. \par
\par
The logical calls to the Logical Console Overlay are determined within the P?S/8  Kernel as described below.\par
\par
Bit[0] of 07607 if set means that the FCA is located in x5400-x5777. If this is  clear, it is then the virtualized contents of two very low system blocks TBD, but  will be very much adjacent to the blocks that handle the file operations equivalent  to the OS/8 User Service Routine [known in P?S/8 SHELL as the User File Operator  [UFO].]  Timing tests have been performed that prove that as long as the two FCA  blocks are quite close to the UFO blocks, performance will be surprisingly close to  being resident in memory despite any perceived conventional wisdom.\par
\par
Note: This is the principal reason why OS/8 CANNOT JUSTIFY REQUIRING 8K! [This is  the topic of an alternate post, too far afield here to continue, etc.]\par
\par
Thus, if bit[0] is clear, the UFO components will use the contents of the two  hardwired low system blocks instead of location x5400-x5777.\par
\par
Bits[1-6] of 07607 are the high-order bits of the 18-bit date value. [Note: As  discussed above, this will first be made available in the next full release, P?S/8  Version 9A.] As of P?S/8 version 8Z, bits[0 through 6 are PRESUMED to be always  0000000, and minor changes to all programs will be made to isolate bits [7-11].   [Note: This generaly consists of adding an AND (37) instruction to the once-only  initialization code within all relevant system programs.]\par
\par
Bits[7-11] are the count of how many passed file handles are output files.  P?S/8  passes as many as 17 [octal 0021] file handles.  All could be output files, or all  could be input files, or any mixed situation.  These bits provide the count of how  many are output files [if any].  The rest [if any] are input files.\par
\par
Location 07610 is the low-order system date word.  There is an "imputed" high-order  section as described above.  In Version 9A the format will be changed so the 18  bits of 07610 and bits [1-6] of 07607 form an 18-bit resident date in the range of  the years 1900 through 2411.  Any value is valid within this range.  [Note:  Far  future versions of P?S/8 could in theory change the base year :-) .]\par
\par
The internal format of 07610 is quite different in Version 8Z, and is essentially a  presumption of 31 days in every month running for 11 years + etc.  Dividing the  date word by 372 gives the relative year in the range of 0 through 10.  By  convention, the partial relative year 11 is to be avoided; the intention is to  update the imputed year every 8 years thus, there is a three-year "grace period" to  allow more than adequate time for updating the relevant words [as documented in the  P?S/8 Patch log file].  This describes all programs that calculate the date for  printing purposes [if at all], and all should be updated at once.  The release tape  of P?S/8 Version 8Z on TC01/TC08 DECtape is already patched to support 2016 through  2027.  P?S/8 Version 9Z is expected quite early within that worst-case time-frame!\par
\par
Location 07611 contains several inter-related parameters:\par
\par
Bits[0-2] are the actual physical size of the machine where 0 is 4K and 7 is 32K by  memory fields.  Unlike OS/8, no program has to calculate the memory size; it is  available in the system kernel as described here.\par
\par
Bits[3-5] are the LARGEST value that the current logical core size is allowed to be  set to.  OS/8 supports a similar concept, but it is not comprehensive enough to  support a Logical Console Overlay.\par
\par
This field can only be the same as the field described in bits[0-2] maximum.  If  the logical console overlay is not active AND there is no system handler device  extension present, the two values can coincide.  If the Overlay is present, this  value generally is the amount available which is one less than the physical size in  bits[0-2].  However, this is NOT how it is defined.\par
\par
In theory, the Logical Console Overlay could occupy more than 0000-5377 in the  highest field of memory.  In that case, the logical calls go to the highest memory  field, but in theory more code of the Overlay could be in one or more prior fields.  [Note: An obscure peripheral is the VK8A.  It would appear it takes two memory  fields to support it, but we have no documentation; it was apparently a successor  to the VT8E with support for upper-lower case and 132 column mode, etc. With that  large a character buffer, the terminal emulator would likely be far more code than  for the smaller VT8E screen, which resembles a 64-column wide VT05; however, output  automatically creates obvious continuation lines for those cases that are wider  output, etc.]\par
\par
Thus, the field to call is the same as the one described in bits[0-2] but it is NOT  a given that the field bits [in 3-5 are necessarily one less field, but to date  this is always the case.  Conforming programs must use this value as the highest to  be used if desired.]\par
\par
Bits[6-8] are the CURRENT logical core size.  Unlike OS/8, 000 means the highest  logical field is field 0 on a 4K system.  [In OS/8, 0 just means it is not  defined].\par
\par
The value can be 000 up to and including the contents of bits[3-5].  The user can  set this with the CORE xxx command in the P?S/8 system programs directory.  Valid  arguments are 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, and ALL.  All settings are scrutinized for  consistency and validity within the current configuration.  ALL is a request to get  the most allowed, etc.  The CORE command also includes a display of all three  values as currently set, etc.\par
\par
Bit[9] is set if the System Device Handler extension is in effect.  By examining  all of the above bits, conforming programs can dynamically configure how much  memory is available. If only 4K, this implies 00000-07577.  Whole additional memory  fields may also be available, and/or a partial memory field foreshortened as  determined above.  In the basic P?S/8, the only practical restriction is at most  the last 1/4 memory field is reserved [or if the Logical Console Overlay is  enabled, none of the topmost memory field is available.]\par
\par
Bit[10] is set if the Logical Console Overlay is in effect.  The CIF instructions  associated with the calls are determined by bits[0-2] and use the field one  smaller. [As stated above, this MIGHT NOT be the same as the value in bits[3-5].\par
\par
Bit[11] is the logical abort bit.  Conforming programs running on PDP-8 models need  not use this, but are free to do so.  DECmate systems MUST use this bit to exit  with a Control-C abort in effect as the keyboard flag is lost due to the  incompatible hardware of the DECmates.]\par
\par
to set a logical abort, perform ISZ I [7611] before exiting to 07600.\par
\par
As described elsewhere, location 07632 is reserved for a multi-purpose embedded  call to the system device handler.\par
\par
When a true system program is run, location 07635 contains the starting block of  the program.  This allows true system programs to calculate the blocks for internal  overlays, etc. The function word in location 07634 indicates the logical unit the  program was loaded in from as well, etc.  Location 7637 contains the starting  address [which should implicitly be known as part of the program design].\par
\par
As in OS/8, system programs might be chained to, in which case, they actually start  one higher, but this information is not relevant here.  For example, P?S8 PAL may  optionally chain to P?S/8 MAP and/or P?S/8 BIN to either map then load the binary  output of the assembly according to passed command-line option switches, etc.  BIN  is capable of realizing it is being chained to by MAP and whether this is in turn  from PAL or not using inter-program conventions shared by the three programs.\par
[P?S/8 does not dictate any of this],\par
\par
7756 - the passed =xxxx parameter value.  If set to 7777, it was not specified; the  program can decide what the defaults are.\par
\par
7757-7777.\par
\par
Passed file handles.  As determined in the low-order five bits of 7607, the first  files [if any] are output files followed by input files [if any].  Lists exactly 21  octal long are not terminated, as clever use of the ISZ I instruction will  automatically self-terminate.  For all other possibilities, the list is terminated  by a word of zero to indicate the end of all passed files.\par
\par
File handle format.\par
\par
In the R-L Monitor, there was no special alignment of the starting block for the  TFS files.  In theory, any reasonable block could be used, etc.  However, the R-L  Monitor was only ever implemented for a single DECtape drive.  As such, there was  no logical unit support.\par
\par
In P?S/8, there is support for the logical unit; as is the case with most system  device configurations, this means drives 0 through 7 [which in the case of the  TC01/TC08 DECtape and other devices] are the actual DECtape drives, which can be  set by the user by the appropriate manual setting on the front panel of the drives,  etc.  [Note: For some larger devices, logical units refer to a mapped arrangement  on a physical device.  In some cases, there may be several choices each with a  different set of tradeoffs, etc.]\par
\par
Since TFS files are of a fixed size of 16 [20 octal] blocks, the imposition of a  boundary requirement simplifies the logical block implementation.  As such, the  starting block of the TFS directory is constrained to a multiple of 8 blocks.  \par
Note: All current implementations are actually demanding needless constraint to a  multiple of 16 blocks; however, this never yielded any actual feature.  As such,  the constraint of every 8 blocks is now the official limitation.  The notion, never  implemented is superseded by the addition of the P?S/8 SHELL that will be part of  the Version 9 releases, etc.\par
\par
Thus, the starting block number of any TFS file is stated in the nine highest bits  of the file handle; the low-order three bits is the logical unit of the file.   These values are easily isolated with AND instructions using the appropriate  constants [7770 for the block number and 0007 for the logical unit].  This is the  basics of file access for any TFS file in the basic system.\par
\par
Note: When used as temporary files, % and $ qualify as they are created on a  boundary of 16.  The "file" used as part of the /I option of P?S/8 BIN to induce  the system device handler to be reloaded, is permanently assigned to block 0100,  thus this also is in proper alignment.\par
\par
Extended-length files.\par
\par
Originally, there was an intention to support multiple files, each of which were of  potentially different lengths, to all reside in high block numbers, such as those  found on larger storage devices.  This feature was never fully implemented and  remains as an overhead to be removed in the next release.  However, it does serve  to provide one important piece of information:  The highest block that can be  accessed by the system device handler on any of the logical units.  This can be as  high as block 7777 in theory, but many devices are smaller than this maximum.\par
\par
Note: Certain devices are actually even longer.  The blocks 1000 and higher cannot  be accessed by the system device handler.  P?S/8 supports non-system device  handlers that can access as much as 2048 times as much storage as OS/8 on each of  the logical units!  This corresponds to 32 GB assuming six-bit bytes, etc.\par
\par
This feature is under development and is an outgrowth of an implemented subset for  the sole benefit of the BLKCPY program, which can copy or compare any number of  blocks between devices.  It is thus, the "stopgap" in development between the  current system and that of Version 9A.  [In version 9A, the implementation of the  device handlers and descriptor table is being revamped; the actual handlers will  easily achieve the claimed size.  In the case of many devices such as RX01,  TC01/TC08, TC12 LINCtapes, etc. the current handlers will only require minor  modifications to support special calls that are being formulated currently.  For  example, although the maximum size of even an extra-long DECtape is only 3300 octal  blocks [easily achieved in any version of the proper handlers], blocks about  77770000 will be reserved for special functions.  An attempt to access block  77770000 will cause the handler to determine the actual maximum size of the  currently mounted DECtape.  This can be done by searching forward until an END zone  error; the last block successfully transferred before the END zone error is the  highest block available.  This is needed to handle DECtapes of differing sizes.  In  OS/8 there is a rudimentary analog for the possibility of a single-density diskette  in an RX02 drive as opposed to a double-density drive.  However, only those two  cases are handled and only as a special case for RX01/RX02 and requires an obscure  newer handler.  In P?S/8, every device will potentially support the feature.  An  error return means the drive is not ready, and in the case of the TC01/TC08 this  can be achieved by also timing out the done flag, etc.  The actual length will be  stored into the caller's buffer in the first two words in the format of the highest  block allowed.  [Note: This is actually the number that is the origin one capacity  of the drive, but actually means the first block that CANNOT be accessed.  Block  0000 is always assumed to exist in all structures; if the value is 0000 this means  4096 blocks are present.  Returned values for existent DECtapes will be 00002702  and 00003000 etc.  Larger devices will return values meaningful enough to require  the double-precision.  For example, P?S/8 support for the RK8E is configured to  access the entire drive.  In terms of 128 12-bit words, the capacity of each  logical drive is from blocks 00000 through 31277.  As such, the RK8E equivalent  function will return 31300.]\par
\par
Thus, for most existent non-system handlers, the only change is to support double- precision block numbers [which for some devices ignores the high-order bits  entirely] and support the special functions [such as the current media length] etc.\par
\par
For the present, the scope of the non-system handlers is identical to the system  handlers, and the main purpose is to allow data interchange only [or to compare  blocks on dissimilar devices],  Thus, this is not a very large change as most of  the facility is already implemented, etc.\par
\par
And of course, all of the above is a prerequisite for the implementation of the\par
P?S/8 SHELL which will embrace the larger storage capabilities, etc.\par
\par
Since extended-length files are under the purview of the system device handler, the  largest theoretical block is 7777.  This is only meaningful on larger devices such  as the RK8E.\par
\par
P?S/8 supports the OS8CON conversion utility.  This program can convert between P? S/8 text files and OS/8 text files in either direction.  By specifying the /E  command-line option switch, conversion of P?S/8 files from OS/8 input can be  accomplished to extended-length output files.\par
\par
Each logical unit can handle an extended-length file [one each] assuming each  logical drive is configured functionally for the purpose. [Note: Each logical unit  can be configured differently.  For example, by limiting the TFS storage and system  program storage on any device, it is possible to start extended-length files from  approximately block 0160 throu\f1 g\f0 h the maximum size of the device, etc.  On some  larger devices, a substantial P?S/8 basic system can coexist with extended-length  files further up to the device through as much as block 7777.  On RK8E systems,  this is routinely the case, etc.\fs20\par
\par
\par
