    work known as \ul\i The R-L Monitor\i0  \i System\ulnone\i0  which was created as a result\par
    of an incredible amount of manual labor over the course of a four\par
    month period.  No conventional software tools were used as the\par
    environment that existed at the time didn't include any.\par
\par
    The front panel of the particular PDP-8 machine used for this work\par
    was quite thoroughly worn out from toggling in thousands of\par
    instructions; it would not become reliable again until (among\par
    other things) it was repaired by replacing all of the front panel\par
    slide switches by Charles Lasner, the author of this document and\par
    principle architect of much of P?S/8.\par
\par
    Note: All of the code in The R-L Monitor System was all new work\par
    created during that time period with the exception of a binary\par
    copy of DEC's \i PAL III\i0  assembler.  While normally meant for use\par
    with paper-tape, PAL III was modified to read the contents of\par
    files on the R-L system DECtape, and to create binary output\par
    written to a designated area on the system DECtape.  (The R-L\par
    Monitor System is a fixed closed-end project available only for a\par
    single DECtape drive such as a TU55 or TU56H).\page     While unconfirmed, it is generally accepted that the particular\par
    image of PAL III was obtained from a copy of the \ul\i DECtape Library\par
\ulnone     \ul System\ulnone\i0  which was provided by DEC when the TC01 and TU55 were\par
