\par
2) DEC management was convinced that it was necessary to market an\par
   operating system that required 8K memory.  (The decision was not\par
   made by consulting expert programmers; the only known input came\par
   from marketing people unconcerned with technical details.)  While\par
   it may be true that certain programs might either require or take\par
   advantage of extended memory, it is not technically valid the\par
   operating system need require 8K as well.\page    Note: While it is known this "executive decision" was made circa\par
   1969, within five years DEC was selling PDP-8/A models which could\par
   be configured with less than 4K system memory depending on the\par
   application.\par
\par
   Customers seeking small PDP-8/A-based solutions were encouraged to\par
   discuss their requirements; certain small memory modules were\par
   marketed to best meet the specialized needs of this customer base.\par
   An operating system that did not require extended-memory hardware\par
   to perform certain program execution tasks (as opposed to software\par
   development) would be a welcome product to this market segment.  It\par
   is a much harder to sell an operating system that requires 8K just\par
   to perform simple 4K (or less) program loading tasks to such\par
   customers.\par
\par
   The notion of the differing requirements of the operating system,\par
   and certain programs that run under the operating system, is easily\par
   demonstrated using P?S/8.  Depending on the specifics, a program\par
   may issue error messages if the requirements are not met.\par
\par
   For example, the P?S/8 \i PAL\i0  assembler might run in 4K, but not under\par
   all circumstances.  Due to the large number of modular options, it\par
   is not possible to enable all of the available options and also\par
   load the complete set of permanent symbols (which includes optional\par
   sections used only if dual-mode assembly of PDP-8 and LINC\par
   programming is enabled); however, there is a remedy in the form of\par
   an acceptable tradeoff: a command-line switch reconfigures the\par
   input and output buffers used during the assembly process; this\par
   increases the available symbol table storage space sufficiently to\par
   load all optional symbol table sections, but significantly slows\par
   the entire assembly process.\par
\par
   The result technically allows all features to be accessed, but at\par
   the expense of only a small number of user symbols; as such, this\par
   means that the size of the source code is likely too large for any\par
   practical assembly.  As such, it is highly likely the assembly is\par
   aborted due to symbol table overflow.\par
\par
   As a result, the minimum amount of memory actually needed is a\par
   function of the requirements of the user and not that of the\par
   operating system.  For many users, forcing a needless requirement\par
   of 8K memory is quite problematic, especially when for many models,\par
   adding in the extended memory controller and additional memory\par
   stacks is either very expensive or quite difficult or both.\par
\par
   Had competent programmers had any input to these decisions, perhaps\par
   the outcome would have been quite different from what actually\par
   transpired.  The experience with the LINC-8 and PDP-12 operating\par
   systems didn't gain them any insight into this very real problem.\page    Because of the separation of the overall group of PDP-8 programmers\par
   who knew each other into "factions" there wasn't sufficient\par
   communications to solve this problem properly.\line\line    There are several key issues as to what the proper scope of the\par
   system device handler must accomplish for a successful operating\par
   system as follows:\line\line    a) The system device handler must be located within locations\par
      07600-07777.  This is an absolute requirement as this last page\par
      of field 0 (the highest page of a 4K machine) has several pre-\par
      existing requirements created during the prior paper-tape-only\par
      era.  Clearly, exit to the operating system must be made by\par
      restarting at location 07600.\par
\par
      This was first established in the earlier \ul\i DECtape Library System\ulnone\i0  \par
      which was used to save images of memory in the range of\par
      00000-07577 (or any subset memory space to the nearest complete\par
      memory page).  This system allows saving images of binary paper-\par
      tapes loaded into memory (which is a tedious and error-prone\par
      process).  Named images can be quickly loaded into memory at any\par
      time to eliminate waiting for paper-tape loading, etc.\par
\par
      Additionally, the highest page of field 0 may have as many as\par
      six locations reserved for three-cycle data break.  As such,\par
      many restrictions are placed on the usage of these locations.\par
      (07750-07751 are reserved for the DF32/RF08, 07752-07753 are\par
      reserved for the TC58 magnetic tape controller and 07754-07755\par
      are reserved for the TC01/TC08 DECtape controller.)\line\line    b) Subroutine calls must be available to read or write multiple\par
      blocks of memory to designated groups of storage blocks on the\par
      device.  While there are no fixed design rules, earlier works\par
      used separate read and write entry points; unfortunately, this\par
      causes avoidable overhead; memory locations for code space or at\par
      an absolute premium.  Inevitably, certain compromises must be\par
      made; simplifying the implementation of any aspect of the design\par
      is highly desirable.\par
\par
      Fortunately, some aspects of this were hammered out by members\par
      of the group at large.  The notion of a composite function word\par
      is used to supply the basic read or write function, the number\par
      of memory pages involved (1-31; 32 is implied if this field of\par
      three bits is set to all zeros), extended memory field for the\par
      data transfer (to support up to 32K memory) and, in the case of\par
      P?S/8 only, three logical bits to represent the individual drive\par
      units as first implemented in the earlier type 552 DECtape\par
      controller for the PDP-5 (that was occasionally used on early\par
      PDP-8 systems).\page       Note: The time frame of these early design decisions included\par
      the TC01 DECtape controller and the TU55 DECtape drive; each\par
      drive has manual logical unit settings for any drive in the\par
      range of 0 through 7.  The earlier type 552 DECtape controller\par
      and type 555 dual DECtape drives were still supported by DEC in\par
      such as existing Typeset-8 systems with the clear intention of\par
      phasing them out in favor of the TC01 and TU55 (later TU56 dual\par
      drives).  However, the 552 controller also supports eight drives\par
      numbered 1 through 8.  Proper documentation can clear up any\par
      confusion when using one controller instead of the other.  Three\par
      bits accommodate the logical unit bits for both of these\par
      controllers (as well as similar aspects of other tape\par
      controllers such as the TC12 on the PDP-12).\line\line       When DEC management was forcing their notions on the in-house\par
      programmers, the notion of the logical unit was not yet\par
      developed; it was assumed by all of the WCFMPG members that the\par
      quick and dirty demo program would be scrapped in favor of the\par
      newer design as kicked around by all members of the P?S as well.\par
\par
      Because of the incredibly stupid edict of the incompetent\par
      managers to only use the demo code, the logical unit feature was\par
      never implemented.  As such, P?S/8, as a 4K operating system, is\par
      more powerful in this regard than the 8K system that was\par
      eventually marketed as OS/8.\par
\par
      Comparing P?S/8 and OS/8 system device handler calls, the\par
      function word of both systems is identical with the exception of\par
      the logical unit bits, which in OS/8 are largely undefined.\par
\par
      Note: In the interest of complete accuracy, the lowest of the\par
      three bits that were meant to be logical unit bits is\par
      tentatively reassigned in OS/8 as follows:\par
\par
      Within all existing OS/8 programming, there are perhaps as many\par
      as four calls to the system device handler that set this low-\par
      order bit.  This is used to inform an ignorant system device\par
      handler implemented on DECtape or LINCtape the notion that it is\par
      certain it is faster to initiate a forward initial block search\par
      on the tape rather than the usual default reverse search.  This\par
      helps per se, as any real opportunity to avoid needless reverse-\par
      first searches will speed up all such tape operations.\par
\par
      However, this is actually a fallacy:  While it is true that most\par
      searches should be started in the reverse direction, as many as\par
      40% of actual searches would benefit from initial forward\par
      search.  Unfortunately, most of these opportunities can only be\par
      determined dynamically.\page       The P?S/8 TC01/TC08 DECtape system device handler includes a\par
      small coding section that determines the proper initial search\par
      direction based on comparing the last block transferred during\par
      the previous call to the handler with the latest block specified\par
      in the current call.  (An overshoot factor must be included to\par
      avoid false positives,)\line\line       Moreover, there exists a user-written replacement for the OS/8\par
      system device handler that incorporates the same logic.  (It was\par
      copied from the P?S/8 system handler source code.)  Similar\par
      techniques can be used for the TD8E DECtape controller; due to\par
      excessively "smart" hardware, the TC12 LINCtape controller\par
      cannot be made to perform anything but an automatic reverse\par
      initial search.\par
\par
      Given all of the above, this minor frill is actually inferior to\par
      the easily implemented dynamic search method; as such, the usage\par
      of this bit is superfluous and can be considered as an\par
      unimplemented feature equivalent to the other two bits which\par
      were never used.\par
\par
      Note: Had the demo been scrapped, the design of the system would\par
      have taken a different course.  In OS/8, each logical device is\par
      a one-off item.  The handler for DTA0: and the one for DTA1: are\par
      totally independent.  In P?S/8 the non-system handler is for the\par
      DTA device and the command syntax includes placing the logical\par
      unit number into the commands as needed.  As such, the single\par
      handler covers all eight logic units in the range of 0 through\par
      7.\line\line       Had OS/8 been designed to use the same conventions, there would\par
      be a SYS0: through a SYS7: notation with SYS: reserved for\par
      accessing the drive booted to.\par
\par
      Note: The P?S/8 TC01/TC08 system device handler is compatible\par
      with an extended bootstrap for any logical unit in the range of \par
      0 through 7.  (All systems can be booted on drive 0; only P?S/8\par
      can handle the rest of the logical drive settings.)  As such,\par
      P?S/8 implements this (although the usual command syntax is\par
      slightly different).\line\line       OS/8 implemented on the RK8E supports the same notion of an\par
      extended bootstrap.  However, instead of embracing the logical\par
      unit openly, the limited syntax causes command anomaly.  Unless\par
      the user is aware of the drive unit chosen, handlers can access\par
      different logical drives than expected, etc.  Implementing\par
      logical drives in the system device handler could eliminate any\par
      of these anomalies.\page    c) Other functions that can be implemented in the system device\par
      handler include writing out memory to a scratch area before\par
      loading the system keyboard monitor and implementation of an\par
      embedded call to the system device handler totally contained\par
      within the 07600-07777 system kernel area.\par
\par
      These two features interact in various ways and introduce\par
      tradeoffs.  While the OS/8 design includes the memory saving\par
      feature, this was implemented in an ill-advised way.  The demo\par
      version of the code was too expedient to see the folly of the\par
      specific implementation.  As such, not enough of memory was\par
      saved to allow more general functionality.  (This was copied\par
      from the poor design of The Disk Monitor as demanded by the\par
      idiot managers.)  By being slavish to this notion, additional\par
      overhead had to be managed by segmenting memory areas to the\par
      nearest 1K of memory (including the use of memory locations in\par
      field 1).  By needlessly segmenting memory, the system kernel\par
      must include control words to manage this, etc.\par
\par
      Additionally, as all programs exit to location 07600, this means\par
      that memory is \b always\b0  saved regardless of the user's\par
      requirements.  This is inherently dangerous as the bootup\par
      process may be error-prone.  If the device is write-protected,\par
      it is beyond the ability of an typical end user to realize the\par
      error is caused solely by the write protect hardware and not a\par
      consequence of the boot process gone awry.  Allowing the system\par
      to continue from a HLT condition may corrupt the operating\par
      system!\line\line       In actual practice, many users either ignored this potential\par
      deadly problem, or were never made aware of the issue.  There\par
      are periodic reports of actual monitor head destruction of the\par
      OS/8 bootup media, including potential destruction of the device\par
      directory, etc.\par
\par
      While further discussion of this important issue is needed, it\par
      is important to first discuss the implementation of basic\par
      program execution.\line\line       Because of the schism between the WCFMPG programmers and the P?S\par
      programmers, the WCFMPG (now DEC employees) were out of touch\par
      with the proper solution to this problem as implemented in\par
      P?S/8.\line\line       The WCFMPG implementation of the R-L Monitor System has a major\par
      design flaw.  There is no way to execute programs directly!\par
      Instead, as necessary, the system device handler is removed from\par
      memory and replaced with what was known as the "system loader"\par
      code.\page       The R-L Monitor System first loads into memory a template copy\par
      of the system loader.  All parameters to be passed to the\par
      executable are copied to the proper relative position in the\par
      template.  This also includes patching in the starting block of\par
      the program (as obtained from the system programs directory) and\par
      the designated starting address.  Input files specified by the\par
      user are also passed into the corresponding reserved area as\par
      well as the reserved location for the =\i xxxx\i0  specified on the\par
      command-line.\par
\par
      The system loader includes reboot code for the TC01/TC08 for\par
      logical drive unit 0.  (This code is compatible with many other\par
      PDP-8 operating systems.)\par
\par
      There are no subroutine calls; the only function is to read in\par
      the executable program up to 00000-07577.\line\line       When the executable program is started, the system parameters\par
      are available, but there is no means to functionally make use of\par
      them; the vital system device handler is not present.\line\line       The "solution" in the R-L Monitor is to require all system\par
      programs to include their own internal means of accessing the\par
      system DECtape, etc.  This generally took the form of a stand-\par
      alone subroutine that was compatible with the actual system\par
      device handler (which is only available to the keyboard monitor \par
      routines).\line\line       In effect, this extra burden means the executable program cannot\par
      quite be as intended.  For example, in R-L FOCAL, the internal\par
      pointers of FOCAL are modified to protect the memory locations\par
      occupied by the read/write subroutine included in the program.\par
      As such, R-L FOCAL cannot support the full breadth of programs\par
      that function in the original paper-tape implementation of\par
      FOCAL, 1969.\par
\par
      The P?S/8 solution to this problem was to implement an embedded\par
      subroutine call within the system kernel followed by two\par
      instructions to start the program after loading.\line\line       Note: It was extremely difficult to accomplish this!  A lot of\par
      effort was expended squeezing down the code of the system device\par
      handler.  Arguably, this problem was the largest example of\par
      brainstorming the squeezing down of any program on any computer\par
      architecture ever.\line\line       The PDP-8 architecture allows many forms of code-space\par
      optimization; details of this advanced subject are beyond the\par
      scope of this document.  However, the current P?S/8 TC01/TC08\par
      system device handler does implement this call (honorarily also\par
      named the system loader as was called the longer routine in The\par
      R-L Monitor System).\page       Program execution is achieved by merely inserting the parameters\par
      obtained from the system programs directory into the\par
      corresponding in-line arguments of this embedded call to the\par
      system device handler.\par
\par
      Note: Unlike the "system loader" described above in The R-L\par
      Monitor System, this increases the reliability of the execution\par
      process; error recovery is now completely at parity with the\par
      capability of the system device handler.  (The R-L routine\par
      merely halted on any error and the only recovery possible is to\par
      reboot at 07600; the command execution is lost.)\par
\par
      Most importantly, there is no need for system programs to\par
      internally support a read/write subroutine in lieu of the system\par
      device handler; rather, calls can be made to the system device\par
      handler which remains resident throughout the entire execution\par
      process; the former code space reservation is now eliminated.\par
\par
      The specific nature of the system device handler need not be\par
      known by the program.  This is referred to as complete device-\par
      independent execution.  The typical P?S/8 system program is\par
      written for any release of P?S/8 regardless of the specifics of\par
      the system device configuration.\par
\par
      The equivalent function in the OS/8 demo program was not\par
      implemented.  An underlying reason was the lack of\par
      communications between the WCFMPG and the P?S/8 programmers; the\par
      advanced P?S/8 implementation was thus ignored out of\par
      expediency.\line\line       As such, and in light of the management edict of requiring 8K,\par
      the demo design was compromised.  Thus, OS/8 requires the\par
      reservation of 17600-17777 as well as 07600-07777.   The OS/8\par
      equivalent call to the system handler is located within\par
      17600-17777 to accomplish the equivalent program execution\par
      loading; however, the size of the resident system kernel was\par
      doubled despite no overall justification.\line\line       While there are other parameters stored in the field one area, a\par
      better design doesn't require any of them.  Moreover, a\par
      significant number are wasted merely being used as part of the\par
      bootup process.\par
\par
      Note: P?S/8 does not use a separate block devoted to little more\par
      than the bootup process.  Despite the lack of this frill, P?S/8\par
      runs on virtually every device OS/8 does (often requiring less\par
      minimum memory).  The design of the bootup process requires more\par
      effort, but always yields a successful design.  This is true\par
      despite certain designs not being mindful of systems such as\par
      P?S/8 where there could be considerations that don't arise in a\par
      system with a separate bootstrap totally apart from the main\par
      device handler code.\page       Starting with the next complete release of P?S/8 (Version 9A),\par
      certain small modifications are being made to accommodate the\par
      upcoming major upgrade, the P?S/8 SHELL.  This optional\par
      component will provide a superior operating system environment\par
      when compared to OS/8; equivalent system calls will be part of\par
      this support, allowing P?S/8 to execute slightly modified\par
      versions of any and all OS/8 programs.  This will be\par
      accomplished without reserving any additional memory per se.\line\line       Note: Certain OS/8 programs cannot be moved to the P?S/8 SHELL\par
      environment because they are only concerned with OS/8-specific\par
      implementation particulars; as such, they are either entirely\par
      not required, or P?S/8 equivalents will be written to\par
      accommodate the superior environment, etc.\line\line       P?S/8 operates using an advanced memory model: Field 0\par
      requirements for both systems are identical, albeit the specific\par
      system kernel parameters within the memory range of 07600-07777\par
      are quite different between the two systems.\line\line       In the case of system parameters related to file operations,\par
      P?S/8 operates by one of two methods:\par
\par
       i) Location x5400-x5777 contains the analog to the OS/8 file\par
          parameters as used in the OS/8 \ul\i User Service Routine\ulnone\i0 .\par
          However, the P?S/8 equivalent known as the \ul\i File and\par
\ulnone           \ul Communications Area\ulnone\i0  (\i FCA\i0 ) is far larger; sections of the FCA\par
          will be defined as existing features of the P?S/8 SHELL as\par
          presently being implemented.  Additional memory words are\par
          reserved for future expansion.\line\line           A portion of the FCA is reserved for file-related parameters\par
          as required by the \ul\i User File Operato\ulnone\i0 r (\i UFO\i0 ).  All SHELL-\par
          related system programs will obtain services from the UFO.\par
          In the case of migrating programs from OS/8, the existing\par
          USR-related routines will be upgraded to UFO compatibility.\par
          In general, there should be little to no additional\par
          modifications required (although some will be made to \par
          eliminate weaknesses and bugs, etc.).\line\line           The P?S/8 system kernel includes all required information on\par
          how to interface with the UFO, including determining if the\par
          non-resident method is in effect (as described in ii)\par
          below).\page       ii) The contents of the FCA are accessed by reading or updating\par
          the contents of two adjacent system blocks located near the\par
          beginning of the current operating system blocks.  Exact\par
          block placement has not been determined pending other\par
          features and block reassignments that will be determined\par
          before P?S/8 Version 9A is released.  These blocks will be\par
          physically near the storage blocks for the UFO itself,\par
          allowing only minimal performance degradation as compared to\par
          the memory-resident implementation as described in 1) above.\par
\par
          Note: The present method of access to the FCA is determined\par
          by a bit within the P?S/8 system kernel.\par
\par
          Present plans for the UFO implementation is as an offset\par
          past a basic system program to be called SHELL in the system\par
          programs directory.  SHELL operation can be enabled and\par
          disabled from the basic system.\par
\par
          If the SHELL is present, this should be an early entry in\par
          the System Programs Directory.\par
\par
          Overall system performance is expected to be superior to\par
          OS/8 when running programs that inherently need only 4K\par
          memory or less.  Programs requiring 8K or more can benefit\par
          from the elimination of the reservation of the last page of\par
          every memory field (other than the highest physical field of\par
          memory, if applicable).\line\line           Note: In the case of migrating the OS/8 BASIC and FORTRAN IV\par
          runtime systems, the P?S/8 SHELL environment provides a\par
          superior memory layout for the benefit of these 15-bit\par
          addressing programs.  Where relevant, the contiguous memory\par
          space can start at location 10000; in OS/8, this must start\par
          at 20000, thus wasting an entire memory field.\par
          Additionally, the kludges associated with OS/8 system device\par
          handlers that require 12K is completely eliminated, as are\par
          the inherent implementation risks.  During talks associated\par
          with the never-completed negotiations with DEC management to\par
          take over the P?S/8 project, the P?S/8 memory model was\par
          discussed with the DEC PDP-8 programming staff; they agreed\par
          that the P?S/8 method was superior.  However, management\par
          would never approve changing their stagnated design from\par
          years earlier.\par
\par
          By implementing an embedded call to the system device\par
          handler in field 0, P?S/8 entirely avoids the need to\par
          consider saving an image of memory.  P?S/8 always boots up\par
          without writing; as such, the system device can be write-\par
          locked for improved safety.\page           While OS/8 always writes out part of memory to scratch\par
          blocks, rarely is this functionality needed.  Memory saving\par
          is the designated way to create system programs, but this\par
          can be accomplished by alternate means.\line\line           When P?S/8 loads programs in binary form (as opposed to\par
          core-image executable form), the contents of the embedded\par
          call to the system device handler are defaulted to save all\par
          of field 0 in the designated full-field scratch area (blocks\par
          0020-0057).  As such, a program that requires being saved\par
          can accomplish this by instead starting at the entry point\par
          of the embedded system device call (07632).  After all of\par
          field 0 is saved, the embedded call restarts the normal\par
          read-only bootstrap process at 07600.\line\line           The P?S/8 \i GET\i0  command accomplishes this automatically.  (It\par
          is actually an alias to the P?S/8 \i BIN\i0  utility specifying the\par
          complete set of parameters to perform the memory save\par
          operation and then restart, etc.\par
\par
          As such, a user program can be created using the GET\par
          command; additional utilities are available to start the\par
          created temporary image program, debug it and optionally\par
          save it in binary file format for deferred loading.\line\line           Note: The basic P?S/8 system does not currently suppport any\par
          utilities to directly create an additional system program\par
          (or delete an existing one).  Advanced users are capable of\par
          performing this using existing utilities.  Future releases\par
          may include such utilities for the basic system, but this is\par
          a very low priority.  However, the SHELL environment will\par
          include a system program builder of higher complexity than\par
          OS/8.  This is necessarsy because all P?S/8 executable\par
          programs can directly support overlays; this is cannot be\par
          accomplished merely by saving loaded memory locations.\par
\par
          Part of the problem of system component creation will be\par
          overcome by a more useful implementation of the \i DTORG\par
         \i0  directive in P?S/8 PAL.  Instead of being a useless and\par
          perverted feature originally implemented to accommodate the\par
          Typeset-8 group, it will be repurposed into a general-\par
          purpose feature to define code overlays, etc.  This will be\par
          incorporated into the SHELL image building utility and may\par
          provide a stop-gap method of creating user-written system\par
          programs for the basic system.\line\line           Also planned is the P?S/8 \i BINCON\i0  program to allow OS/8 users\par
          the ability to take advantage of all features of P?S/8 PAL\par
          (including the corrected DTORG directive).  Because of the\par
          overall importance of this issue, documentation is available\par
          elsewhere as part of the specification of the BINCON\par
          utility.\page\par
